\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a finite set $X$, let $F(X)$ be the free group on $X$. A presentation $\langle X\mid R\rangle$ denotes $F(X)/\langle\!\langle R\rangle\!\rangle$, where $R$ is a finite set of relator words and double angle brackets denote normal closure. In the source's terminology a presentation is finite when its presented group is finite, and it is just finite if deleting any one specified relator produces an infinite group.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does every finite group $G$ admit a presentation $\langle X\mid R\rangle\cong G$ such that
\[
 \bigl|F(X)/\langle\!\langle R\setminus\{r\}\rangle\!\rangle\bigr|=\infty
 \qquad\text{for every }r\in R?
\]
The words in the other relators and the generating set are kept fixed when a relator is removed. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10402v1}{[S3]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every finite group be presented so that removal of any single relation makes the resulting group infinite?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2519 (KOU-21.10). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Y. Barnea, Problem 21.10, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records an affirmative answer; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\item[S3] Marc Lackenby, \emph{Every finite group admits a just finite presentation}, arXiv:2605.10402v1 (2026), Introduction and Theorem 1. The theorem gives an affirmative answer with finite generating and relator sets. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10402v1}.
\end{description}
